description: In Clojure an extension that names a protocol extends it, methods or not; native let-go counts a protocol as extended only once one of its methods follows it. JVM Clojure 1.12 and the pinned native lg, on 2026-10-30:
 
     (defprotocol Named (nm [this]))
     (defprotocol Marker)
     (extend-type java.lang.Long Named Marker (nm [n] n))
     (satisfies? Named 1)   ; Clojure true, let-go false
     (satisfies? Marker 1)  ; both true
     (nm 1)                 ; both raise: nm sits under Marker
 
 and the same for (deftype T [] Marker) and (extend-type String Marker): true in Clojure, false in let-go. A marker protocol (no methods), the usual way to tag a type, never satisfies in let-go.
 
 The llvm lane's satisfies? (corpus/poly/protocols.lg) follows native today; the cases above are left out of the corpus until this is decided, so no test holds either answer.
 
 Facts that close it:
 - F1 native lg answers as Clojure does for the three shapes above.
 - F2 corpus/poly/protocols.lg holds them, matching native on llvm.
